export const debate = {"id":"mcdowell-ehrman-moral-objectivity-2026","number":"38","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Sean McDowell vs Bart Ehrman: The Moral Argument, Guilt, and the Teachings of Jesus","label":"Moral objectivity and Christian ethics","date":"2026-08-23","duration":"1 hr 04 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cz57BuQOCmQ","motion":"Do Jesus' teachings and the West's moral transformation point to objective moral truths grounded beyond human preference, or can morality be explained as historically shaped human inclination without divine grounding?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"McDowell presses objective obligation and progress; Ehrman explains universal charity historically while separating human moral experience from mind-independent grounding.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"Do we have obligations to care for the poor","context":"McDowell grants the worldwide historical transformation but asks whether it represents genuine improvement and creates obligations rather than inclinations."},"con":{"text":"Jesus universalized this so that it would be applied to anybody in need","context":"Ehrman identifies Jesus's distinctive contribution as extending assistance beyond family, friends, nationality, ethnicity, race, and social membership."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Moral realist","speaker":"Sean McDowell","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Secular historian","speaker":"Bart Ehrman","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":86,"con":82},"sections":[{"sectionId":"objective-truth-and-access","title":"Objective Truth and Human Access","timebox":"10:03–17:36","score":{"pro":81,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-objective-mind-independent-facts","time":"10:03","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Moral objectivism means that moral facts remain true independently of anyone's beliefs, preferences, or attitudes, comparably to mind-independent historical and scientific facts.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move provides a precise and recoverable definition of moral objectivism, separating truth from individual belief, preference, and attitude while using familiar historical and scientific comparisons to clarify the intended independence relation. Principal limitation: As a reply, this definitional clarification does not contact the decisive historical claim that Jesus's teaching caused Western charity, and it does not itself establish either moral facts or reliable access to them. Live burden: The argument must connect the intelligible concept of mind-independent morality to positive grounding and explain why rejecting divine grounding need not be reduced to individual-opinion subjectivism. Locked score: The strong/competent band recognizes conceptual clarity, stable terminology, appropriate calibration, and central relevance, while retaining the substantial responsiveness limitation created by leaving the historical target unanswered.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-subjective-access-to-objectivity","time":"12:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Even if objective moral values exist, people can recognize them only through subjective human cognition and therefore possess no unmediated access to moral objectivity.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The conditional formulation grants objective value for argument's sake and sharply identifies the missing epistemic step between mind-independent truth and human knowledge, directly challenging the practical consequence of McDowell's definition. Principal limitation: Establishing that cognition is mediated by human brains does not establish that the resulting access is unreliable, inadequate, or uniquely problematic, because historical and scientific knowledge is likewise cognitively mediated. Live burden: The objection must identify a relevant reliability deficit in moral cognition rather than relying exclusively upon the universal fact that human judgment occurs through subjective faculties. Locked score: The strong/competent band reflects exceptionally direct contact, stable distinctions, and diagnostic importance, tempered by an inference from necessary mediation toward epistemic limitation that remains somewhat stronger than its evidence warrants.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-domain-specific-moral-access","time":"15:44","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Different subjects can require different methods, so moral truth need not be accessed like physical matter; morality concerns immaterial truths rather than scientific objects.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply correctly blocks an inference from different methods of access to the absence of truth and uses the accepted methodological differences between history and physical science to motivate domain-sensitive moral inquiry. Principal limitation: Calling moral truths immaterial assumes part of the disputed ontology, while the move never specifies a positive method for accessing them or demonstrates that such a method produces reliable judgments. Live burden: McDowell must explain what moral cognition consists in, how its reliability can be assessed, and why the answer addresses Ehrman's concern about unavoidable subjective mediation rather than an unasserted demand for scientific measurement. Locked score: The strong/competent band recognizes incisive diagnostic contact and a coherent domain distinction, while reflecting the underdeveloped epistemology and implicitly assumed metaphysical premise.","tags":[]},"con":null}]},{"sectionId":"moral-commonality-and-variation","title":"Cross-Cultural Commonality and Historical Variation","timebox":"26:15–38:55","score":{"pro":82,"con":77},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-enduring-principles-expanded-scope","time":"32:36","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Cross-cultural moral principles can endure while practices change, with Jesus expanding their application toward poor, disenfranchised, and outsider populations rather than originating morality itself.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The principle-application distinction provides a coherent reconciliation between historical transformation and enduring morality, allowing Jesus's contribution to expand the scope of concern without requiring that every underlying moral principle began with him. Principal limitation: The claims that common principles existed across cultures and \"from the beginning\" remain historically broad, while the move does not show that earlier principles genuinely entailed later outgroup assistance rather than receiving retrospective reinterpretation. Live burden: McDowell must identify stable principles with representative cross-cultural evidence and explain the inferential bridge from those principles to universal charity. Locked score: The strong/competent band recognizes a useful conceptual distinction and relevant partial answer, while reflecting underqualified historical scope, limited support, and the continuing gap between commonality and objectivity.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-late-emergence-of-universal-aid","time":"26:15","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Treating assistance to hungry and homeless strangers as a central moral priority appears historically recent rather than evident throughout most of Homo sapiens' existence.","score":71,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move supplies a concrete instance of historical variation by contrasting a personally central commitment to aiding hungry and homeless strangers with the claimed absence of that priority across most human history. Principal limitation: The enormous temporal generalization extends far beyond the representative historical or archaeological evidence presented, and absence from surviving evidence cannot straightforwardly establish absence from human concern or practice. Live burden: Ehrman must define the relevant priority, distinguish institutional visibility from moral recognition, and substantiate the claim across heterogeneous societies without conflating limited documentation with nonexistence. Locked score: The mixed band reflects genuine relevance to historical contingency and a clearly identified value, alongside sweeping scope, inadequate displayed support, and failure to answer the immediate normative challenge concerning standards for progress.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-commonality-comparative-explanation","time":"37:24","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Pre-Jesus cross-cultural commonality answers the alleged historical inconsistency and leaves a comparative question about which explanation best accounts for shared moral beliefs.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply carefully narrows the immediate claim, using preexisting commonality to answer alleged inconsistency while presenting realism as a comparative explanation rather than claiming that similarity deductively proves objectivity or theism. Principal limitation: Posing the comparative question does not resolve it, and the move supplies neither discriminating evidence nor developed rival models showing why objective grounding explains common beliefs better than evolutionary, historical, or social processes. Live burden: McDowell must specify predictions or explanatory virtues that distinguish his account and address substantial cultural differences alongside the asserted common core. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent calibration, justified reframing, explicit qualification, and direct engagement with Ehrman's non-entailment objection, while recognizing that the preserved abductive case remains programmatic rather than completed.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-commonality-does-not-entail-objectivity","time":"35:07","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Cross-cultural similarities coexist with substantial differences and therefore do not demonstrate either objective morality or the claim that a superior being bestowed moral understanding.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move accurately separates observations of cross-cultural similarity from the stronger inference to objective or divinely bestowed morality, and its limited denial of demonstration is appropriately calibrated. Principal limitation: Merely rejecting entailment does not answer whether commonality supplies abductive evidence for realism, nor does the excerpt develop an alternative historical, evolutionary, or social explanation for the observed similarities. Live burden: Ehrman must compare rival explanations and identify evidence that favors a naturalistic account rather than treating the absence of deduction as the absence of evidential relevance. Locked score: The very strong band reflects direct diagnostic contact, stable scope, careful limitation, and a successful challenge to an overstated inference, while leaving the broader comparative-explanation question substantially open.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"jesus-and-universal-charity","title":"Jesus and the Universalization of Charity","timebox":"1:26–43:20","score":{"pro":92,"con":86},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-jesus-charity-objective-dignity","time":"28:20","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Jesus's introduction of a universal ethic does not make morality subjective; care for poor people can instead rest on their objective dignity as bearers of God's image.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply grants Jesus's historical role, correctly denies that historical novelty logically entails subjectivity, and presents a coherent positive grounding for universal concern in the value and dignity of persons made in God's image. Principal limitation: The divine-image proposal is asserted rather than independently defended, so the move explains the realist position without yet demonstrating that this grounding is preferable to secular accounts of human worth. Live burden: McDowell must support the theological premise and show how it produces determinate obligations while acknowledging that late emergence can remain evidence about recognition even without settling metaethics. Locked score: The very strong band reflects comprehensive contact, explicit distinctions, charitable treatment of the historical evidence, and a substantive grounding proposal, moderated by its undeveloped theological warrant.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-jesus-charity-historical-cause","time":"1:26","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Jesus taught an unusually universal responsibility to help unrelated people in need, and sustained Christian influence subsequently embedded that ethic within Western moral consciousness.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The historically focused causal thesis directly supports the motion by identifying Jesus's universalized charity as the proposed origin of a consequential transformation in Western moral consciousness, while the phrase \"so far as we can tell\" appropriately acknowledges historical uncertainty. Principal limitation: The selected evidence supplies little comparative documentation for the unusually broad claims about earlier philosophers, Jewish tradition, or the many intervening centuries through which this influence supposedly operated. Live burden: The account must distinguish Jesus's contribution from antecedent traditions and demonstrate a credible transmission pathway from teaching to durable social conscience. Locked score: The strong/competent band reflects a clear, relevant, cautiously expressed thesis whose considerable historical scope remains incompletely substantiated within the presented support.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-charity-genuine-improvement-duty","time":"42:19","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A worldwide ethical transformation becomes genuine moral improvement only if caring for poor people is obligatory, rather than merely an inclination produced by historical circumstances.","score":94,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply generously grants the magnitude of Jesus's worldwide historical transformation and then isolates the motion's decisive normative question by distinguishing descriptive change from genuine improvement and obligation from inclination. Principal limitation: Framing the question powerfully exposes a burden but does not itself establish that objective morality is necessary, because a naturalistic account might ground evaluation relative to shared needs, agency, or human purposes. Live burden: McDowell must explain why such internal standards cannot support genuine duties and then positively connect objective value to obligations concerning strangers and poor people. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding relevance, precision, calibration, charitable concession, and motion-level diagnostic force, while recognizing that this interrogative move identifies rather than completes the required grounding argument.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-jesus-universalized-outgroup-care","time":"39:24","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Jesus distinctively universalized assistance beyond kin and social groups, making anyone in need an appropriate recipient regardless of nationality, ethnicity, race, friendship, or family.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move states the proposed historical innovation with exceptional precision by enumerating family, friendship, nationality, ethnicity, race, and social-group boundaries that no longer restrict who should receive assistance. Principal limitation: The selected excerpt does not provide sufficiently comparative evidence from Jewish, Greco-Roman, or other antecedent traditions to establish that this universalization was historically distinctive in the breadth claimed. Live burden: Ehrman must document both meaningful precedents and discontinuities, then explain the mechanisms by which Jesus's formulation acquired enduring influence beyond the immediate movement. Locked score: The very strong band reflects a clear, consequential, motion-level constructive with stable scope and concrete content, while the slight overstatement in its distinctiveness claim preserves an important historical evidentiary burden.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"inclination-obligation-and-progress","title":"Inclination, Obligation, and Moral Progress","timebox":"22:30–49:44","score":{"pro":90,"con":79},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-objective-standard-for-progress","time":"23:18","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Claims that beliefs are superior or historical changes constitute moral progress require a standard beyond contingent evolved inclinations if those judgments are categorical rather than preference-relative.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move exposes a consequential standard-of-comparison problem by placing Ehrman's inclination-based account beside his reported descriptions of superior beliefs and moral progress, then asking what could authorize those evaluations. Principal limitation: The apparent inconsistency depends upon interpreting \"superior\" and \"progress\" categorically, although both expressions could instead describe improvement relative to endorsed human purposes such as reducing suffering or expanding cooperation. Live burden: McDowell must show why purpose-relative evaluation is insufficient for the claims at issue and provide a positive account of how an objective standard grounds comparisons without merely restating objectivity. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise diagnostic pressure, direct central engagement, and considerable normative importance, with an implicit interpretive condition preventing the challenge from becoming conclusive.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-inclination-without-objective-standard","time":"22:30","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Human inclinations can present conduct as unquestionably right or wrong, but that psychological certainty does not demonstrate an objective standard beyond human inclination.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The concise distinction between the force with which an inclination presents itself and the metaphysical status of its content correctly denies that felt certainty alone entails a mind-independent moral standard. Principal limitation: Although it contacts McDowell's definitional distinction, the response leaves unanswered his more recent domain-specific proposal and offers no developed account of why some inclinations deserve endorsement when they conflict. Live burden: Ehrman must supplement the non-entailment point with an epistemic and normative account explaining how human inclinations can be evaluated, prioritized, or corrected without presupposing external authority. Locked score: The strong/competent band reflects a logically proportionate denial, stable terminology, and meaningful central contact, while preserving the responsiveness deficit created by bypassing the latest methodological challenge.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evolutionary-feelings-lack-authority","time":"46:15","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Evolution can explain deep moral feelings causally, but its contingency alone cannot make them authoritative, binding, or better than competing inclinations without an additional normative principle.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely separates evolutionary causal origin from normative authority and identifies two decisive consequences: contingency alone cannot explain why one feeling outranks another or why either becomes binding. Principal limitation: The argument defeats an appeal to evolution by itself but does not exclude naturalistic normativity constructed through rational reflection upon agency, cooperation, suffering, or stable human needs, a qualification the move appropriately leaves open. Live burden: McDowell must demonstrate that every adequate supplementary bridge requires mind-independent or divine grounding and then defend his preferred source of authority positively. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, conceptual stability, proportionate force, and direct engagement with both human-nature and inclination claims, without crediting the limited insufficiency argument as a comprehensive refutation of naturalism.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-human-nature-grounds-moral-action","time":"43:18","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Built-in inclinations are constitutive of shared human nature, not merely superficial feelings, and can therefore ground moral belief and action without an external standard.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response usefully rejects the dismissive characterization of inclination as \"merely\" subjective and identifies deeply rooted, constitutive human nature as a serious source of moral motivation, belief, and practical action. Principal limitation: The term \"grounds\" shifts among causal explanation, motivational capacity, and normative justification, leaving unexplained why constitutive inclinations possess authority or how conflicting inclinations should be ranked. Live burden: Ehrman must articulate a bridge from stable human needs or agency to justified norms, including procedures for criticism, conflict resolution, and progress beyond whichever impulse happens to prevail. Locked score: The mixed band reflects a plausible motivational insight and meaningful partial contact, but also substantial conceptual instability and failure to answer the central comparative-authority challenge directed at inclination-based morality.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"guilt-practice-and-explanatory-scope","title":"Guilt, Moral Practice, and Explanatory Scope","timebox":"59:44–63:14","score":{"pro":84,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-guilt-real-code-forgiveness","time":"59:44","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Guilt, conduct that presupposes a real moral code, and the perceived need for forgiveness may be better explained by objective wrongdoing than by inclination alone.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move introduces familiar moral phenomena into a comparative explanation and explicitly invites an alternative account, connecting guilt, realist-seeming practice, wrongdoing, and forgiveness to the broader grounding dispute. Principal limitation: The concepts of guilt, sin, moral code, and forgiveness combine distinct psychological, normative, and theological claims, while the phenomenology underdetermines whether its explanation is realist, evolutionary, cultural, or mixed. Live burden: McDowell must separate these phenomena, establish their relevant features, and show why objective wrongdoing explains them better than adaptive social emotions and learned communal norms. Locked score: The strong/competent band reflects a relevant and recoverable constructive challenge with comparative potential, tempered by conceptual compression, implicit qualifications, limited discriminating evidence, and an explanatory conclusion stronger than the presented observations uniquely support.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evolutionary-account-of-guilt","time":"61:01","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Guilt may have evolved because people disposed against harming their communities survived more successfully, allowing those dispositions and associated genes to propagate.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response directly supplies the requested rival explanation and carefully avoids purposive evolutionary language, describing differential survival of guilt-prone individuals whose behavior reduces damaging conduct within their communities. Principal limitation: The proposed selection history and genetic specificity are plausible but unsupported in the excerpt, and an adaptive origin for guilt can coexist with guilt sometimes tracking objective wrongdoing rather than excluding realism. Live burden: Ehrman must present evidence for the mechanism, distinguish biological from cultural transmission, address forgiveness separately, and explain what evidential comparison would favor the non-realist interpretation. Locked score: The strong/competent band recognizes a responsive, coherent, mechanistically specified alternative that weakens explanatory uniqueness, while preserving limitations in empirical warrant and in the inference from an available causal account to broader metaethical consequences.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-transformation-worldview-explanation","time":"62:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Agreement on a large historical moral transformation leaves a broader comparative question: which worldview best explains that transformation and its connection to morality?","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply accurately accepts Ehrman's historical and scope clarifications while preserving a distinct abductive question about what best explains the acknowledged transformation, thereby avoiding any claim that history deductively establishes a worldview. Principal limitation: \"Worldview\" remains broad, and raising a comparative question supplies neither explicit alternatives nor discriminating criteria by which the historical and evolutionary explanations could be evaluated against objective moral grounding. Live burden: McDowell must formulate rival explanatory models, identify predictions or virtues relevant to the transformation, and show why theological realism contributes more than the historical causal account already provides. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, charity, precision, and motion-level relevance, while recognizing that the well-calibrated question establishes an agenda for comparison rather than completing that comparison.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-history-independent-of-metaethics","time":"62:19","role":"Concession or qualification","words":"Jesus's historical influence can explain transformed ethical sensibilities without resolving moral objectivity or requiring belief in God, because the historical thesis operates independently of metaethics.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The scope clarification is explicit and appropriately modest, preserving the historical transformation thesis for believers and nonbelievers while refusing to claim that historical influence by itself settles objectivity, subjectivity, or divine grounding. Principal limitation: Logical independence does not establish evidential irrelevance, because the same historical facts might still contribute to a wider abductive comparison among worldviews or explanations of moral development. Live burden: Ehrman must distinguish a self-sufficient historical explanation from the separate question of whether its evidence changes comparative confidence in competing metaethical accounts. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent calibration, justified reframing, stable terminology, and legitimate limitation of the historical argument, notwithstanding its deliberate nonresponse to McDowell's independent challenge about the normative authority of evolved inclinations.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":86,"strengths":["McDowell repeatedly distinguished descriptive historical transformation from genuine moral improvement, keeping obligation and normative authority at the center of the motion.","His strongest replies granted Ehrman's historical claims before explaining why novelty, cognitive mediation, or evolutionary origin does not by itself decide moral objectivity.","The challenge from contingent evolutionary feelings to comparative authority directly contacted the con account's weakest bridge and carefully limited itself to insufficiency without denying every naturalistic supplement.","McDowell reframed cross-cultural commonality and historical transformation as comparative explanatory evidence rather than presenting either observation as a deductive proof of theism."],"blunders":[{"text":"The proposed access route classified moral truths as immaterial without supplying a positive method for knowing them, leaving the epistemic defense substantially incomplete.","links":[]},{"text":"The guilt argument compressed psychological guilt, objective wrongdoing, theological sin, and forgiveness into one explanatory package that the presented evidence did not uniquely support.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":82,"strengths":["Ehrman clearly specified Jesus's universalization of outgroup care and connected it to a historically consequential transformation without claiming that history settled metaethics.","The subjective-access objection directly exposed the missing epistemic step between defining mind-independent facts and establishing dependable human knowledge of moral truth.","His evolutionary account of guilt supplied a genuine alternative mechanism and thereby weakened the claim that guilt uniquely indicates an objective moral code.","The late scope clarification carefully separated a historical thesis available to believers and nonbelievers from stronger conclusions about objectivity or divine grounding."],"blunders":[{"text":"The appeal to constitutive human inclination did not distinguish causal motivation from normative justification or explain how conflicting inclinations acquire comparative authority.","links":[]},{"text":"The claim that universal aid was absent through most human existence had sweeping temporal scope without representative historical or archaeological support in the locked evidence.","links":[]},{"text":"Rejecting a deductive inference from cross-cultural commonality did not answer McDowell's narrower claim that commonality might carry comparative explanatory weight.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that objective moral realism offers the stronger integrated explanation when universal human worth, categorical obligation, rational criticism of prevailing inclinations, and genuine moral progress are considered together.","premises":["Historical novelty and changing application are compatible with objective truth because communities can recognize a valid principle incompletely, apply it selectively, and later correct exclusions without creating the principle through agreement.","An evolutionary explanation of moral feelings describes why agents possess motivations, but categorical authority requires an additional account of why a judgment remains binding when it conflicts with advantage, preference, or dominant convention.","Fallible cognitive mediation does not uniquely discredit moral knowledge, provided moral inquiry can use consistency, impartiality, experience, factual correction, and reflective comparison to identify and revise unreliable judgments.","Grounding equal dignity in a source beyond fluctuating human approval can explain why strangers retain worth even when a society, ruler, or majority refuses to acknowledge their claims."],"conclusion":"Taken together, these premises support treating objective grounding as a serious comparative explanation of universal dignity and obligation, while leaving its theological and epistemic premises open to further argument."},"newArguments":[{"title":"The dissident criticism test","text":"A moral framework should explain how a dissident can correctly condemn a settled social practice before collective preferences change. If justification is exhausted by actual inclination or convention, the dissident may oppose society but cannot yet say society is mistaken in a standard-independent sense. Objective realism explains the dissident's posture by distinguishing what people approve from what their equal worth requires. This does not prove realism, but it identifies a comparative advantage concerning criticism under hostile consensus."},{"title":"Convergence through error correction","text":"Partial cross-cultural convergence matters most when it results from recognizable error-correcting practices: widening whose testimony counts, exposing factual misconceptions, testing consistency, and applying accepted protections impartially. Realism can interpret such convergence as improved recognition rather than mere synchronization of preference. The claim remains comparative because social learning can also explain convergence; its force depends on whether the direction of change is best understood as discovering implications already supported by reasons that exceeded local approval."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that historically developed human morality can sustain reasoned obligation and criticism through shared vulnerability, agency, reciprocity, and social practices without first resolving mind-independent moral ontology.","premises":["Humans share durable needs for bodily security, cooperation, dependable expectations, social recognition, and opportunities to exercise agency, even though cultures interpret and prioritize those needs differently.","Norms can be justified through reciprocal procedures asking whether rules are factually informed, consistently applied, attentive to affected people, and reasonably acceptable among agents who must live under them.","Evolutionary and cultural histories can explain capacities for guilt, empathy, and cooperation without determining which present norms should survive reflective scrutiny or implying that every evolved impulse deserves endorsement.","Jesus's universalization of aid can represent a major historical improvement relative to defensible human aims because it widened reciprocal concern, reduced arbitrary exclusion, and addressed severe avoidable need."],"conclusion":"These premises support a secular account on which universal charity is historically caused, reflectively justified, and criticizable through shared human reasons, without claiming that this account disproves objective morality."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Public justification under disagreement","text":"A practical moral standard gains authority when people with different metaphysical beliefs can examine the same reasons, challenge factual errors, and demand consistent treatment. Universal aid can be defended publicly through vulnerability, avoidable suffering, reciprocity, and the arbitrariness of excluding strangers. This authority is not mere popularity: majorities can fail the procedure by suppressing affected voices or applying reasons selectively. The account therefore permits criticism and reform without requiring prior agreement about God or mind-independent properties."},{"title":"Genealogy without debunking","text":"A historical or evolutionary genealogy need not prove a norm false; instead, it can reveal which parts of moral judgment arose from parochial pressures and which survive broader reflection. Expanding the circle of concern may be defended because exposure to outsiders corrects false beliefs about their capacities and makes selective exemptions harder to justify consistently. This model treats moral progress as improved information and reciprocal coherence, not simply as the replacement of one prevailing feeling by another."}]}}};
